{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on the kernel-package question 2356b681 (evidence-rules slice\na53e1464, draft 33c3cb46ad), 28 October 2026. Rests on the question,\non the slice request and on ruling c20152ef. Rulings ed818781,\nf73805e4 and c20152ef stand; point 1 of c20152ef is read as below.\n\n1. What c20152ef point 1 forbids. No native timed projection port, no\n   fold or replay rule, no new kernel concept. That is the whole of\n   it. The point was written against the timed port; it was not\n   written against the rules observation record, which ed818781 had\n   already made the mark's home. Both rulings are the planner's.\n\n2. Permitted, as a kernel addition under docs/kernel.md section 7:\n   the optional members roomService (a ServiceMark or null) and\n   serviceGeneration (a ScopedRulesPosition) of RulesContent in\n   contract/observation.ts; contentStates and contentChecked in\n   derive/observes.ts stating both exactly when the definition's\n   data declares the roomServiceChanged reference; and the matching\n   record shape in bytes/records.ts. This is the rules observation\n   record's next revision, after rows I3-36 (singleControllerException,\n   revision 19) and I3-43 (extents, revision 20): the same form, the\n   same exactness check, earlier definitions have neither member.\n   ServiceMark and ScopedRulesPosition stay in contract as the two\n   type-only interfaces that RulesContent needs; put them beside\n   RulesContent or leave them in contract/evidence.ts, your choice.\n\n   Why the kernel and not a platform service: the observation of the\n   rules is the kernel's form for what one scope's judge may know of\n   the Rules scope, and the validator checks the record exactly\n   against the definition's data (contract 16.1). ed818781 chose an\n   admin-published mark with a native-selected position scoped to\n   the exact Rules incarnation. The record states that position; a\n   value retained under the extents digest cannot prove it on its\n   own, the kernel would read it blind, and extents (repository\n   layers) would carry a checker identity it was never for. That\n   envelope is a special case of the kind section 4 lists. So the\n   literal alternative you recommend is refused; keep the draft's\n   additions as they are.\n\n3. Not permitted in contract: EvidenceBinding, CheckSource,\n   CheckStage and CheckSubject. They are the Destination's job\n   binding, platform vocabulary, and kernel.md section 5 (K4) lists\n   contract's platform vocabulary as the thing to retire, not to\n   grow. Move them to packages/platform (beside\n   destination-evidence-v17.ts) or packages/checkers, whichever\n   imports the other today, with no kernel package importing them.\n   The binding codec bytes/src/evidence.ts (domain\n   artroom-check-binding-1) goes with them, as K3 moved the\n   application codecs to platform; it keeps using canonicalBytes,\n   digestBytes and the ref checks that bytes exports. bytes keeps\n   no file for it.\n\n4. The head's review cites docs/kernel.md section 7, names the\n   addition as the rules observation record's revision 21 in the\n   place where revisions 19 and 20 are noted (the records.ts comment\n   and the contract's 16.1 text), and gives point 2's reason. It\n   closes no K item; this ruling is the planner's hold decision in\n   advance, so the head is not held for it. Section 3 of kernel.md\n   does not change. The checker checks the rule on the head as\n   usual.\n\n5. Unchanged: no timed form or port, no fold, replay or custody\n   change, no change to any historical grammar (contentChecked is\n   exact per definition, so Rules 12 and earlier observations stay\n   as they are), and the witnesses of c20152ef point 7 as listed.\n\nRetire the question when you have read this. Component validation\ncontinues from 33c3cb46ad.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}